cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it together with the archived dataset listed under identifiers."
type: software

title: "Scripts for Rapid Parameter Estimation from Photoluminescence Decays of Halide Perovskite Thin Films"
abstract: >-
  The dataset entails synthetic physics simulations and experimental photoluminescence measurements 
  by HEUMANN, RUDOLPH, HUANG, KIRCHARTZ and DREESSEN (Forschungszentrum Jülich), 
  supporting neural-network-accelerated Shockley–Read–Hall (SRH) trap characterisation of semiconductor thin films. 
  The basic methodology is described in 
  "Heumann et al. (2026): Rapid Parameter Estimation from Photoluminescence Decays of Halide Perovskite Thin Films , arxiv,  	
  https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2609.18438". 
  The dataset contains scripts using Python toolsets for extracting trap parameters from  time-resolved (TRPL) and 
  steady-state (SSPL) photoluminescence measurements of semiconductor thin films using Shockley–Read–Hall recombination models with radiative recombination included. These scripts provide CMA-ES parameter fitting with an optional neural-network surrogate
  for the transient solver, and a Bayesian grid pipeline that maps the posterior
  over the full parameter space and reports credible intervals.
  At its core, the dataset provides a large synthetic training corpus of differential carrier lifetime (τ_diff) vs. quasi-Fermi level splitting (QFLS) curves, 
  computed by numerically solving the SRH transient ODE over a quasi-random Sobol-sampled parameter space and one to two trap levels for the material. Complementing these simulations, the dataset includes experimental time-resolved (TRPL) and steady-state (SSPL) photoluminescence measurements from perovskite thin-film samples, which serve as targets for parameter fitting and Bayesian uncertainty quantification. A further component comprises 1D spatial PDE diffusion simulations (Beer–Lambert excitation profile, film thickness 450 nm, Eg = 1.625 eV) run across five representative physical scenarios and five optical absorption coefficients (α = 10⁴–10⁶ cm⁻¹), from which a homoskedastic model-error sigma (σ_trpl = 0.4936 in log₁₀ space) is derived; this value parameterises the Gaussian log-likelihood in the Bayesian inference stage and accounts for the approximation error of the 0D point model relative to the full 1D diffusion model. Pre-trained neural-network surrogate model artefacts are provided alongside, enabling τ_diff curve prediction at orders-of-magnitude lower computational cost than the ODE solver. The data provided contains relevant variables of the semiconductor photophysical recombination cycle, including trap density (N_t), trap energy level (E_t), electron and hole SRH lifetimes (τ_n, τ_p), radiative recombination coefficient (k_rad), bandgap energy (E_g), carrier excitation density, quasi-Fermi level splitting, differential decay time and photoluminescence quantum yield. (2026)

